  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3}\\le {9}^{2x+1}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\le x$ \n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3} & \\le {9}^{2x+1} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3}-{9}^{2x+1} & \\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3}-{9}^{4}\\cdot {9}^{2x-3} & \\le 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-{9}^{4})\\cdot {9}^{2x-3} & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${9}^{2x-3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({x}^{2}-{9}^{4})\\cdot {9}^{2x-3}}{{9}^{2x-3}} & \\le \\frac{0}{{9}^{2x-3}} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{9}^{4} & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve this inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{9}^{4} & \\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\le {9}^{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{{9}^{4}} \\\\ |x| & \\le {9}^{2} \\\\ |x| & \\le 81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to the inequality above is $-81\\le x<81.$",
